---
import ProjectPageLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectPageLayout.astro';
import SpecTable from '../../components/SpecTable.astro';
import MetricCallout from '../../components/MetricCallout.astro';

const specRows = [
  { label: 'Platform',         value: 'Overactuated UAV' },
  { label: 'DoF',              value: '6'                },
  { label: 'Mass',             value: '155 g'            },
  { label: 'Quadcopters',      value: '4'                },
  { label: 'Quadcopter Model', value: 'Crazyflie 2.1'    },
  { label: 'Gimbals',          value: '4'                },
  { label: 'Motors',           value: '16'               },
  { label: 'Motor Size',       value: '0.28 × 0.6 in'    },
  { label: 'Total Thrust',     value: '0.63 lbf'         },
  { label: 'Frame',            value: 'Carbon Fiber'     },
  { label: 'Gimbal Material',  value: 'PLA (FDM Print)'  },
];
---
<ProjectPageLayout
  title="Omnidirectional UAV Research"
  description="Passive gimbal joint for an overactuated 6-DoF UAV — addressing the mechanical singularity at vertical rotor alignment."
  ghostText="MaCLAB"
  category="Mechatronics & Controls Lab"
  tagline="Overactuated 6-DOF UAV with 4 passive gimbals"
  showOverview={false}
  nextHref="/projects/submersible-v3"
>
  <!-- Overview + specs alongside cropped platform cover -->
  <section class="spec-block" style="grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.5fr; align-items: start; gap: 1.5rem;">
    <div class="spec-col">
      <h2 class="section-overview">Overview</h2>
      <SpecTable rows={specRows} title="Specifications" />
    </div>
    <div class="cover-wrap">
      <img
        class="cover-img"
        src="/assets/projects/maclab/drone_cover.jpg"
        alt="Overactuated UAV — four Crazyflie 2.1 quadcopters in gimbals on a carbon-fiber frame"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="metrics-row" aria-label="Key metrics">
    <MetricCallout value="6"   unit="DoF"    description="Overactuated flight control"      />
    <MetricCallout value="16"  unit="motors" description="Across four gimbaled quadcopters" />
    <MetricCallout value="155" unit="g"      description="Total platform mass"              />
  </section>

  <section class="the-work">

    <!-- ── Problem ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Problem</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          Traditional multirotors are underactuated, so lateral translation
          requires tilting the entire airframe, coupling position and
          attitude. However, this prevents vertical hovering or overhead
          surface inspection. Making a drone fully overactuated solves that
          coupling but creates a new issue: when a gimbaled rotor approaches
          vertical alignment, the mechanism loses an effective degree of
          freedom. Designing a joint that holds the gimbal constrained during
          normal operation and smoothly unlocks an auxiliary axis only near
          this singular configuration, without relying on friction or
          mechanically unpredictable behavior, is the specific problem I am
          working to address.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Concept ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Concept</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          The platform suspends four Crazyflie 2.1 quadcopters in 3D-printed
          (PLA) 2-DOF gimbals mounted on a carbon-fiber frame. This gives each
          actuator the ability to independently steer its thrust vector in 3D
          space. My contribution targets the mechanical singularity: a passive
          joint that constrains the gimbal in its normal operating range and
          unlocks an auxiliary rotational axis when the gimbal approaches
          vertical. The mechanism must transition smoothly, add negligible
          mass, and avoid friction-dependent behavior.
        </p>
        <!-- frame — portrait 3:4, constrain width -->
        <figure class="proj-figure portrait-figure">
          <img src="/assets/projects/maclab/frame.jpg"
               alt="Carbon-fiber frame with gimbal-mounted Crazyflie 2.1 quadcopters and motion-capture markers"
               loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Carbon-fiber frame with gimbal-mounted Crazyflie 2.1 quadcopters and motion-capture markers</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Approach ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Approach</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          Using SolidWorks, I iterate on joint geometry prototypes. Each
          iteration is 3D-printed in PLA and evaluated for transition behavior
          at the vertical angular boundary, structural integrity, and
          compatibility with the Crazyflie mounting.
        </p>
        <!-- gimbal close-up — portrait 3:4, constrain width -->
        <figure class="proj-figure portrait-figure">
          <img src="/assets/projects/maclab/gimbal_close.jpg"
               alt="3D-printed PLA gimbal ring around a Crazyflie 2.1, with a joint iteration in SolidWorks behind"
               loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Printed gimbal ring around a Crazyflie 2.1</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Vision ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Vision</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          This research could lead to future drones that can fly and maneuver
          in all six degrees of freedom, making them useful for inspecting
          cliffs, caves, spacecraft, and other hard-to-reach places. On Mars,
          a scaled version could help explore crater walls, lava tubes, and
          steep terrain that rovers can&rsquo;t safely reach. Its gimbaled
          thrust system could allow a Mars copter to hover while pointing
          cameras or scientific instruments in different directions, rather
          than needing the whole aircraft to tilt. Titan may be an even
          stronger long-term application because its dense atmosphere and low
          gravity make rotorcraft flight easier, allowing similar 6-DOF drones
          to explore icy terrain and organic deposits.
        </p>
        <figure class="proj-figure">
          <video class="media-landscape" autoplay loop muted playsinline
                 aria-label="NASA JPL Mars Chopper concept animation">
            <source src="/assets/projects/maclab/mars.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <figcaption>Mars Chopper, NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>
</ProjectPageLayout>

<style>
  /* Cropped cover image beside the spec table */
  .cover-wrap {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--bg);
  }
  .cover-img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% center;
    display: block;
  }

  .spec-col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .spec-col :global(.section-overview) {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
    font-weight: var(--fw-black);
    color: var(--text-100);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* Portrait photos (3:4) — cap width so they don't dominate the page */
  .portrait-figure {
    max-width: 60%;
  }
  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .portrait-figure { max-width: 100%; }
  }

  /* Mars Chopper video — 16:9 landscape */
  .media-landscape {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
    background: var(--surface);
  }
</style>
